.TH "vsearch-cut" "1" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --cut \[em] use a restriction pattern to cut fasta sequences
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--cut\f[R] \f[I]fastafile\f[R] --cut_pattern
\f[I]pattern\f[R] (--fastaout | --fastaout_rev | --fastaout_discarded |
--fastaout_discarded_rev) \f[I]outputfile\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--cut\f[R] uses a restriction pattern to cut
input fasta sequences.
Input sequences are cut into fragments at \f[B]each\f[R] restriction
site matching the pattern given with the option \f[V]--cut_pattern\f[R].
Restriction patterns are only searched on the forward (or normal)
strand, not on the reverse strand.
.PP
Fragments on the forward strand are written to the file specified with
the \f[V]--fastaout\f[R] file, and the reverse-complement of fragments
are written to the file specified with the \f[V]--fastaout_rev\f[R]
option.
Fragments receive the name of their parent sequence.
Input sequences with not match are written to the file specified with
the option \f[V]--fastaout_discarded\f[R], and their reverse-complement
are also written to the file specified with the
\f[V]--fastaout_discarded_rev\f[R] option.
.PP
A typical restriction pattern is \[lq]\f[V]G\[ha]AATT_C\f[R]\[rq],
representing the EcoRI restriction site.
The nucleotide symbols represent the sequence to be matched.
Lowercase or uppercase nucleotides, as well as ambiguous nucleotides
(IUPAC) are accepted.
See \f[V]vsearch-nucleotides(7)\f[R] for details.
The special character `\[ha]' (circumflex) indicates the cutting
position on the forward strand, while `\f[V]_\f[R]' (underscore)
indicates the cutting position on the reverse strand.
Forward and reverse cutting positions can be the same (for example
\[lq]\f[V]GG\[ha]_AA\f[R]\[rq]), but exactly one cutting position on
each strand must be indicated (one `\[ha]' and one `\f[V]_\f[R]').
.PP
As noted above, restriction patterns are only searched on the forward
(or normal) strand, not on the reverse strand.
For palindromic patterns such as EcoRI, this is not an issue.
A palindromic pattern is identical to its reverse-complement (same
sequence and same cutting positions), so there are no copies on the
reverse strand that do not have a counterpart on the forward strand.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
forward 5\[aq] A...G\[ha]AATT_C...G 3\[aq]
reverse 3\[aq] T...C_TTAA\[ha]G...C 5\[aq]

fragments: A...G, AATTC...G, but also C...G and AATTC...T
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
All copies are detected.
.PP
For asymetrical or non-palindromic patterns, if the pattern appears on
the reverse strand, it will not be detected.
For example, the pattern \[lq]\f[V]GG\[ha]_C\f[R]\[rq] is detected when
present on the forward strand:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
forward 5\[aq] A...GG\[ha]C...G 3\[aq]
reverse 3\[aq] T...CC G...C 5\[aq]

fragments: A...GG, C...G
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
but is not detected when present on the reverse strand:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
forward 5\[aq] A...G CC...G 3\[aq]
reverse 3\[aq] T...C_GG...C 5\[aq]  <- present but not detected
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To detect asymetrical or non-palindromic patterns on the reverse strand,
it is necessary to run the \f[V]--cut\f[R] command on the
reverse-complemented input sequences.
See \f[V]vsearch-fastx_revcomp(1)\f[R] for more details.
.PP
Finally, pattern occurrences can overlap.
For example, the pattern \[lq]\f[V]G\[ha]_G\f[R]\[rq] will be detected
\f[I]three\f[R] times in the sequence \[lq]\f[V]GGGG\f[R]\[rq].
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.TP
\f[V]--cut_pattern\f[R] \f[I]pattern\f[R]
Specify the restriction site pattern (case insensitive IUPAC characters)
and cutting positions (`\[ha]' and \[cq]_\[cq]).
For example, \[lq]\f[V]G\[ha]AATT_C\f[R]\[rq].
See the PATTERN EXAMPLES section for more details.
.PP
At least one of \f[V]--fastaout\f[R], \f[V]--fastaout_rev\f[R],
\f[V]--fastaout_discarded\f[R], or \f[V]--fastaout_discarded_rev\f[R]
must also be specified.
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the forward strand fragments to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta
format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the reverse strand fragments to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta
format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_discarded\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the non-matching sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_discarded_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the reverse-complemented non-matching sequences to
\f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta format.
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--fasta_width\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximal width of sequences when writing fasta files.
Longer sequences are folded and written on several lines.
Default width is 80 nucleotides.
Set to zero (0) to suppress folding.
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--label_suffix\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the suffix \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers when writing fasta
or fastq files.
For example, with \f[V]--label_suffix \[dq];status=healthy\[dq]\f[R],
sequence header `>seq1' becomes `>seq1;status=healthy'.
.TP
\f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
Add a sequence length annotation (\f[V];length=integer\f[R]) to each
sequence header when writing fasta or fastq files.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R]
Retain whole sequence headers in output files.
By default, vsearch truncates sequence headers at first space or
tabulation.
This option suppresses truncation.
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Replace sequence headers with the prefix \f[I]string\f[R] and a ticker
(1, 2, 3, etc.).
For example, with \f[V]--relabel \[dq]cluster:\[dq]\f[R], the first
sequence header becomes `>cluster:1', the second sequence header becomes
`>cluster:2', and so on.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R]
Retain old sequence identifiers by including them at the end of the new
headers, after a space.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the MD5 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The MD5 digest is a 128-bit value (16 bytes), represented using a string
of 32 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]md5(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_self\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the sequence itself.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the SHA1 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The SHA1 digest is a 160-bit value (20 bytes), represented using a
string of 40 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]sha1(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--sample\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the given sample identifier \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers
when writing fasta or fastq files.
For instance, if \f[I]string\f[R] is `ABC', the text
\f[V];sample=ABC\f[R] will be added to the headers.
\f[I]string\f[R] is silently truncated at the first `;' or whitespace
character (space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab or form
feed), so such characters should not be used in \f[I]string\f[R].
Other characters (alphabetical, numerical and punctuations) are
accepted.
.TP
\f[V]--sizein\f[R]
Use the abundance annotations present in sequence headers when reading
fasta or fastq file.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Entries without abundance annotations are silently assumed to be of
\f[V]size=1\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--sizeout\f[R]
Add abundance annotations to sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Add the pattern \f[V];size=integer\f[R].
If option \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is not used, abundance values are set to 1
for all entries.
If \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is used, existing abundance annotations are simply
reported to output files.
.TP
\f[V]--xee\f[R]
Strip expected error (ee) annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]ee=float[;]\f[R].
Expected error annotations are added by the synonymous options
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R] and \f[V]--eeout\f[R] described in
\f[V]vsearch-fastx_filter(1)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--xlength\f[R]
Strip sequence length annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]length=integer[;]\f[R].
Sequence length annotations are added by the \f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
option.
.TP
\f[V]--xsize\f[R]
Strip abundance annotations from sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Abundance annotations are added by the \f[V]--sizeout\f[R] option.
.SS ignored options
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Command is not multithreaded, option has no effect (a warning is printed
when more than one thread is requested).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Cut the sequences in \f[I]query.fasta\f[R], using the restriction
pattern \[lq]\f[V]G\[ha]AATT_C\f[R]\[rq] (\f[V]--cut_pattern\f[R]).
Write the fragments found on the reverse strand to
\f[I]query_fragments.fasta\f[R], in fasta format
(\f[V]--fastaout_rev\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --cut query.fasta \[rs]
    --cut_pattern \[dq]G\[ha]AATT_C\[dq] \[rs]
    --fastaout_rev query_fragments.fasta
\f[R]
.fi
.SH PATTERN EXAMPLES
.PP
(the symbol `|' is used to represent a cut on either strand)
.TP
\f[V]EcoRI\f[R]
use the palindromic pattern \[lq]\f[V]G\[ha]AATT_C\f[R]\[rq] to
represent the following restriction:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
5\[aq] G|AATT-C 3\[aq]
3\[aq] C-TTAA|G 5\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[V]EcoRII\f[R]
use the palindromic pattern \[lq]\f[V]\[ha]CCWGG_\f[R]\[rq] to represent
the following restriction:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
5\[aq] |CCWGG- 3\[aq]
3\[aq] -GGWCC| 5\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[V]EcoRV\f[R]
use the palindromic pattern \[lq]\f[V]GAT\[ha]_ATC\f[R]\[rq] to
represent the following restriction:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
5\[aq] GAT|ATC 3\[aq]
3\[aq] CTA|TAG 5\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[V]Fok1\f[R]
use the non-palindromic pattern
\[lq]\f[V]N_NNNNNNNNNNNNGGATGNNNNNNNN\[ha]N\f[R]\[rq] to represent the
following restriction, with a cut 9 nucleotides downstream of the motif
on the forward strand, and 13 nucleotides downstream of the motif on the
reverse strand:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
5\[aq] -NNNNNNNNNNNNGGATGNNNNNNNN| 3\[aq]
3\[aq] |NNNNNNNNNNNNCCTACNNNNNNNN- 5\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[V]HindIII\f[R]
use the palindromic pattern \[lq]\f[V]A\[ha]AGCT_T\f[R]\[rq] to
represent the following restriction:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
5\[aq] A|AGCT-T 3\[aq]
3\[aq] T-TCGA|A 5\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[V]NspI\f[R]
use the asymetric pattern \[lq]\f[V]RCATG\[ha]_Y\f[R]\[rq] to represent
the following restriction:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
5\[aq] RCATG|Y 3\[aq]
3\[aq] YGTAC-R 5\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
